\section{The two layers are never pooled}{

They answer different questions and are not summands. The between-subject
residual already contains whatever measurement error survived into each
participant's transported value, so adding the within-subject variance to it
would double-count the shared part and still miss the covariance a
variance-components model would need. The record carries \verb{$between} and
\verb{$within} as separate blocks, \code{as.data.frame()} emits one \code{layer} at a time,
and there is no field holding their sum.
}

\section{The \code{t} is uncalibrated, and stays uncalibrated}{

\verb{$between$t} is the estimate over its standard error. Its null distribution
has been \emph{measured} against \eqn{t_{df}}{t_df} by
\code{benchmarks/run-population-null-coverage.R}, a 2,000-replication null
simulation of the group layer. Under a \strong{correctly specified} group model
the nominal 95\% interval covered the null term in \strong{0.9485} of
replications at \code{N = 6}, \strong{0.9500} at \code{N = 8}, \strong{0.9520} at \code{N = 12} and
\strong{0.9480} at \code{N = 24} (Monte Carlo standard error 0.005), and the
Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance between the null \code{t} and \eqn{t_{df}}{t_df} was
at most \strong{0.0209} (\code{p >= 0.34}). The arithmetic is right, which was never
the part in doubt.

The same simulation's second arm is why the label does not move. When each
participant's transported value carries a variance that depends on the group
covariates --- the transport-heterogeneity analogue, since a participant
whose warp is poor is noisier and warp quality is not independent of age,
motion or head size --- coverage of the same interval falls to \strong{0.9230} at
\code{N = 6} and \strong{0.8850} at \code{N = 24}. It gets \strong{worse} with more
participants, because the bias is in the standard error and not in the
sample size: at \code{N = 24} the nominal 5\% test rejects a true null \strong{11.5\%}
of the time, and the null \code{t} is distinguishable from \eqn{t_{df}}{t_df} at
\code{p = 1.2e-6}.

A real population fit carries misspecification of unknown degree and a
transport whose displacement, entropy and subject coverage vary across
participants (\code{population-form-v1} section 7.5), which is exactly the
second arm's regime. Report the statistic; do not report a p-value derived
from it without an argument that those diagnostics are benign.
}

\section{What the within layer is admitted for}{

Participant \code{i}'s transported value at group column \code{u} is a fixed linear
functional \eqn{w'z_i}{w'z_i} of that participant's native query values, so
its variance needs the covariance \emph{between} native nodes. D8 refuses that
object (capability \code{cross_node_sampling_covariance}), so the layer is
admitted exactly where \code{w} has one nonzero entry: there the cross terms
carry weight zero and \eqn{\mathrm{Var} = w_x^2\,\mathrm{Var}(z_{ix})}{Var =
w_x^2 Var(z_ix)} is exact. \strong{No independence assumption is made anywhere},
and none would be defensible: overlapping searchlight supports under
spatially correlated noise are positively correlated, so a diagonal sum
would be an under-estimate in a known direction.

Admission is per participant and per group column, and \verb{$within$admitted}
is the matrix that records it. A hard anatomical parcellation usually
admits nothing, because every group node collects several native nodes;
that is the refusal being visible rather than the layer being broken.

Two gates come before the per-column one, both recorded in
\verb{$within$refusal$reasons} rather than raised:
\itemize{
\item \code{same_data_ratio_normalization} --- a \code{"unit_budget"} population divides
each ledger by a total read from the same data, and section 4.3 records
that the standard error of that divisor does not exist.
\item \code{native_node_labels_unaligned} --- the covariance batch is named by the
nodes \code{rdm_sampling_covariance(at = )} read and the transport by its own
\code{native_index}. Binding two differently named node sets by position would
attach one node's error bar to another's coefficient.
}
}

\section{Refusals}{

Each is an \code{effect_capability_refusal} in namespace
\code{"population_uncertainty"} (see \code{\link[=catch_refusal]{catch_refusal()}}).
\itemize{
\item \code{population_between_subject_residuals} --- a \code{"complete_form"} result.
The streamed route does not retain the participant residuals, by design.
\item \code{between_subject_residual_df} --- a saturated group model. With no
residual degrees of freedom the participants' scatter is not estimable.
}
